WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:05.340
I am fluent in the language of BS,

00:00:07.180 --> 00:00:11.220
otherwise known
as business speak or bullshit,

00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:13.460
depending on your point of view.

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:16.220
What is BS or business speak?

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:20.980
Business speak is the language
we use on the job and only on the job

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:23.660
to describe and define things.

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:26.980
For instance, at home,
I might say to my son,

00:00:27.020 --> 00:00:29.300
"It's time to go. Are you ready?"

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:32.660
While at work, I'm more likely
to say to a colleague,

00:00:32.660 --> 00:00:36.540
"Is it scalable? You can give me
a baseline ETA on departure.

00:00:36.580 --> 00:00:37.900
(Laughter)

00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:39.940
We need to leave ASAP."

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:41.340
Right?

00:00:41.380 --> 00:00:45.900
I was first introduced
to the language of BS through my wife.

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:50.380
She's a chief marketing officer
for a global consulting firm,

00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:53.060
and one day a few years back at home,

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:55.700
she was doing a conference
call on speaker,

00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:59.300
and she started to use the language of BS.

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:01.980
I had never heard this woman
speak this language before.

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:04.820
I was concerned she was a spy.

00:01:04.820 --> 00:01:06.140
(Laughter)

00:01:06.940 --> 00:01:13.180
She used terms like "boil the ocean,"
"tiger team", "SWOT analysis."

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:18.180
Afterwards, she translated
those terms to me

00:01:18.220 --> 00:01:22.300
in phrases that a human being
can actually understand.

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:26.220
And I did what every good partner
does in a solid marriage.

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:28.980
I made fun of those words ...

00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:30.340
(Laughter)

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:31.620
relentlessly.

00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:35.740
I may not make as much money
as my wife's colleagues,

00:01:35.740 --> 00:01:38.020
I certainly don't have
the degrees they do,

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:40.300
but I know a messed up thing
when I hear it.

00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:41.540
(Laughter)

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:43.860
And I devoted years of my life

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:46.420
to compiling, researching

00:01:46.460 --> 00:01:49.420
and then writing a BS dictionary.

00:01:50.420 --> 00:01:52.220
Yes. Thank you. Thank you.

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:58.260
(Applause)

00:01:58.340 --> 00:02:02.900
I can now tell my kids that
an auditorium full of people applauded me

00:02:02.900 --> 00:02:05.660
for all this worthless work
I did over the last few years.

00:02:06.940 --> 00:02:09.140
So in the words of BS,

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:10.820
with this expertise

00:02:10.820 --> 00:02:14.700
I say to you that
my presentation to you today,

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:19.380
the CTA of this DIY
TEDx Talk, if you will,

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:23.260
is a USP on the rise of BS

00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:25.060
in our WIIFM world

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:28.260
and how we can make
this intel less scalable

00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:30.620
during the fourth industrial revolution.

00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:32.180
(Laughter)

00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:34.860
If you don't understand
what I've just said, that's OK.

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:36.140
You don't speak BS.

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:37.220
(Laughter)

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:39.900
If you do understand what I've just said,

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:41.180
God help you.

00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:42.780
(Laughter)

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:45.780
Yes.

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:52.020
(Applause)

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:53.660
So let me give it another shot.

00:02:53.660 --> 00:02:55.940
I'll translate that in more common words.

00:02:55.940 --> 00:02:59.100
The call to action
of my presentation to you

00:02:59.140 --> 00:03:04.300
is to make the point that business speak
is on the rise in our globalized economy.

00:03:04.340 --> 00:03:08.220
But there are simple things
we can all do in our day-to-day jobs

00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:09.500
with that language

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:12.740
to take the bullshit out of BS.

00:03:13.580 --> 00:03:15.780
So what I present to you is three ways

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:19.140
of how to take the BS
out of business speak.

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:24.260
First, you've been sitting for a while,
so let me poll the room.

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:26.220
I'm going to get your feet working here.

00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:28.060
I want you to stomp your feet,

00:03:28.100 --> 00:03:33.020
honestly, stomp your feet
if you know what the BS term EBITDA means.

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:35.100
(Stomping)

00:03:35.140 --> 00:03:36.740
Nice. OK!

00:03:36.740 --> 00:03:39.700
Maybe got some CEOs,
some accountants in the room.

00:03:39.700 --> 00:03:40.980
Never know.

00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:43.340
OK, now stomp your feet if you know

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:47.860
what earnings before interest, taxes,
depreciation and amortization means.

00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:50.220
(Stomping)

00:03:50.260 --> 00:03:51.700
A little bit more.

00:03:51.700 --> 00:03:54.700
To be honest, I'm sure some of you
with amortization were like,

00:03:54.700 --> 00:03:57.340
"I don't know if I should stomp." Right?

00:03:57.380 --> 00:03:59.500
OK, now stomp your feet if you know

00:03:59.540 --> 00:04:03.740
what income is before applying
accounting and tax write-offs to it.

00:04:04.420 --> 00:04:06.260
(Stomping)

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:09.380
That’s a close call, but I’m going
to make a judgment call here.

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:12.060
The loudest stomping in this theater

00:04:12.100 --> 00:04:15.220
got to the last definition
of the same thing.

00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:18.100
EBITDA is basically income

00:04:18.140 --> 00:04:20.620
before you apply accounting
and tax write-offs to it.

00:04:22.340 --> 00:04:24.940
And notice that the most common language

00:04:24.940 --> 00:04:27.420
got the biggest agreement
from this audience.

00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:31.140
But here's the problem with BS.

00:04:31.180 --> 00:04:33.340
It is not a common language.

00:04:34.420 --> 00:04:37.420
Business speak
is the language of the elite.

00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:41.380
Not surprisingly,
when my coauthor and I, Tim Ito,

00:04:41.420 --> 00:04:45.820
researched about 300 business speak terms
for this dictionary,

00:04:45.820 --> 00:04:50.140
one of the recurring themes
is the people who created this language

00:04:50.180 --> 00:04:52.260
and speak it most fluently.

00:04:52.300 --> 00:04:56.580
A large majority of them are people
who look like me.

00:04:57.620 --> 00:04:59.260
A bunch of white guys.

00:05:01.340 --> 00:05:03.060
I say that’s not surprisingly,

00:05:03.100 --> 00:05:06.740
because I want you to think the first
and second industrial revolution,

00:05:06.740 --> 00:05:08.900
when white guys like John D. Rockefeller

00:05:08.900 --> 00:05:11.820
and Andrew Carnegie
ruled the business world.

00:05:11.820 --> 00:05:15.100
And it's true today when research shows

00:05:15.140 --> 00:05:21.900
that 90 percent of all
worldwide Fortune 500 company CEOs

00:05:22.300 --> 00:05:24.300
are white guys, still.

00:05:25.580 --> 00:05:28.460
So here's a takeaway for you in the room.

00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:33.460
If you want to hang
with today's white male tycoons,

00:05:33.500 --> 00:05:35.300
Bill Gates, Elon Musk,

00:05:35.340 --> 00:05:37.180
Jeff Bezos, Warren Buffett,

00:05:37.220 --> 00:05:43.300
it would benefit you to get
very adept and able to speak BS.

00:05:44.740 --> 00:05:46.660
But there's a drawback to that approach.

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:52.020
I have a friend, Emily, who works
at a theatre company in the United States.

00:05:52.060 --> 00:05:54.380
This theater company
does corporate entertainment

00:05:54.420 --> 00:05:56.780
and keynote speeches for conferences.

00:05:56.780 --> 00:05:58.700
She started her job a few years ago

00:05:58.700 --> 00:06:01.620
and had her first big meeting
to try to make a sale.

00:06:01.620 --> 00:06:04.460
It was with a big oil company in Dallas,

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:09.140
and across the boardroom from her
were five old white guys.

00:06:09.180 --> 00:06:11.180
And Emily noticed very early on

00:06:11.220 --> 00:06:14.980
that they did something very interesting
with their language choices.

00:06:15.020 --> 00:06:20.860
They started to use BS words
that they knew she didn't know.

00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:24.460
Now, why would they do that?

00:06:25.700 --> 00:06:29.700
Three possible reasons. I talked to Emily.
We've worked out some theories.

00:06:29.700 --> 00:06:31.820
One, these guys didn't know any better.

00:06:33.380 --> 00:06:37.740
An economist, Robin Hogarth had coined
the term "the curse of knowledge"

00:06:37.740 --> 00:06:39.740
to describe this phenomenon.

00:06:39.740 --> 00:06:42.540
It may be simply that they're
so expert at their language,

00:06:42.580 --> 00:06:47.060
that they don't appreciate anymore
what it's like to not know these things.

00:06:47.100 --> 00:06:49.300
And they just spoke
off the top of their head.

00:06:50.020 --> 00:06:52.340
As a corporate consultant
for public speaking,

00:06:52.380 --> 00:06:54.620
the companies that I work for,

00:06:54.620 --> 00:06:58.780
the reason I give them this advice
is because of that phenomenon.

00:06:58.780 --> 00:07:02.980
I will tell them, If you have
a lot of BS in your four walls --

00:07:03.020 --> 00:07:05.300
and it could be unique
just to your company

00:07:05.340 --> 00:07:07.180
or it's just general acronyms

00:07:07.220 --> 00:07:09.340
and things you use
in the business world --

00:07:09.380 --> 00:07:11.100
and you have a new employee,

00:07:11.140 --> 00:07:14.140
maybe give them a directory
of some of these terms.

00:07:14.180 --> 00:07:17.060
So instead of walking into a meeting
and smiling and nodding

00:07:17.100 --> 00:07:21.220
and then frantically googling afterwards,
what the hell does EBITDA mean,

00:07:22.100 --> 00:07:23.900
that they'll know.

00:07:23.900 --> 00:07:27.340
Second reason why
these five oil executives

00:07:27.380 --> 00:07:29.340
possibly handled Emily this way:

00:07:30.820 --> 00:07:34.700
they wanted to parse out from her
how much they knew --

00:07:34.700 --> 00:07:37.140
how much she knew about the business.

00:07:37.180 --> 00:07:39.540
Instead of asking her directly,

00:07:39.580 --> 00:07:41.220
"Excuse me, Emily, can you tell me

00:07:41.260 --> 00:07:43.380
how much experience
you have in our industry

00:07:43.420 --> 00:07:44.700
and let's go from there,"

00:07:44.700 --> 00:07:48.460
they used BS as a way
to keep Emily and them apart

00:07:48.500 --> 00:07:50.060
rather than bring them together.

00:07:51.140 --> 00:07:55.420
Third possible reason
these oil executives handled it that way:

00:07:55.460 --> 00:07:57.060
they were just jerks.

00:07:57.100 --> 00:07:59.020
(Laughter)

00:07:59.060 --> 00:08:01.500
And Emily thinks that’s the answer.

00:08:01.540 --> 00:08:03.300
(Laughter)

00:08:03.340 --> 00:08:05.820
Because shortly
after her initial conversation,

00:08:05.820 --> 00:08:08.500
she brought in a more senior
male colleague,

00:08:08.540 --> 00:08:11.820
lo and behold, the five
oil executives warm up,

00:08:11.820 --> 00:08:15.940
they cut out the BS, and a deal is struck
shortly thereafter.

00:08:17.780 --> 00:08:20.060
So that's something for us to think about

00:08:20.100 --> 00:08:24.460
as our first takeaway of how
to take the BS out of business speak.

00:08:25.380 --> 00:08:29.500
Use BS to be inclusive and not exclusive.

00:08:30.260 --> 00:08:32.980
In other words, don't be a jerk.

00:08:33.020 --> 00:08:34.820
(Laughter)

00:08:34.820 --> 00:08:38.820
If you can explain something
in common language,

00:08:38.820 --> 00:08:40.100
that's a victory.

00:08:41.020 --> 00:08:43.660
The edit is the genius,

00:08:43.660 --> 00:08:46.140
not spouting off all these big words.

00:08:47.380 --> 00:08:51.780
Which brings me to my second takeaway
of how to take BS out of business speak.

00:08:51.780 --> 00:08:54.100
Let's get those feet stomping again.

00:08:54.140 --> 00:08:55.860
How many of you stomp your feet

00:08:55.860 --> 00:08:58.620
if you know what the word
"de minimis" means?

00:08:59.060 --> 00:09:01.380
(Stomping)

00:09:01.420 --> 00:09:03.500
OK, good. I don't feel so alone

00:09:03.540 --> 00:09:05.860
because I was a like a lot of you

00:09:05.860 --> 00:09:08.300
who did not stomp your feet
a few years back.

00:09:08.340 --> 00:09:09.780
As was mentioned in my intro,

00:09:09.780 --> 00:09:12.300
I used to work for
“The Daily Show with Jon Stewart.”

00:09:12.340 --> 00:09:13.940
For those of you who don't know,

00:09:13.940 --> 00:09:16.860
it is a comedy show
that's nationally broadcast in America

00:09:16.860 --> 00:09:18.940
that satirized the news of the day.

00:09:18.940 --> 00:09:20.780
And I was interviewing an accountant

00:09:20.780 --> 00:09:25.180
about a tax issue that he used
the word "de minimis" to describe.

00:09:25.220 --> 00:09:26.860
And I interrupted him right there

00:09:26.860 --> 00:09:30.180
and I said, "What?
What the F does that mean?"

00:09:31.220 --> 00:09:35.220
And he said, "It means small, immaterial.
It's insignificant."

00:09:35.260 --> 00:09:37.820
To which I said, "Well,
why didn't you just say that?"

00:09:37.820 --> 00:09:39.140
(Laughter)

00:09:39.180 --> 00:09:40.900
And he said in a moment of honesty,

00:09:40.900 --> 00:09:44.140
he said, "I just thought it sounded good."

00:09:44.180 --> 00:09:46.340
(Laughter)

00:09:46.380 --> 00:09:49.980
In reality, it’s kind of
a cool word, “de minimis.”

00:09:50.020 --> 00:09:53.100
And I'm guilty of doing it, too,
especially in a business meeting

00:09:53.100 --> 00:09:55.140
where I'm unsure
of my status in that room.

00:09:55.180 --> 00:09:57.460
I may use EBITDA, right?

00:09:57.500 --> 00:10:00.740
Just to show, "Hey,
I can hang. I can hang."

00:10:00.740 --> 00:10:02.700
(Laughter)

00:10:02.700 --> 00:10:05.260
But here's the thing about communication.

00:10:05.300 --> 00:10:08.220
You know what's really cool
about good communication,

00:10:08.260 --> 00:10:10.460
especially in the business world?

00:10:10.500 --> 00:10:16.660
It's when you can express fresh ideas
without using stale business cliches

00:10:16.660 --> 00:10:20.140
and big words that people in the room
don't really understand.

00:10:20.900 --> 00:10:24.460
So that's my second way to take the BS
out of business language.

00:10:25.340 --> 00:10:26.580
For you.

00:10:27.340 --> 00:10:31.260
To drive this home even further,

00:10:31.300 --> 00:10:33.460
a lady that I worked with
in my corporate work

00:10:33.500 --> 00:10:37.060
works for a big American company,
one of the biggest companies in America,

00:10:37.100 --> 00:10:38.820
if not the world.

00:10:38.820 --> 00:10:41.860
She works for bosses
that use a ton of business cliches,

00:10:41.860 --> 00:10:44.100
so much so to keep herself sane,

00:10:44.140 --> 00:10:48.700
she told me she created
homemade BS bingo cards,

00:10:48.700 --> 00:10:49.820
(Laughter)

00:10:49.820 --> 00:10:51.740
replaced the numbers in the bingo cards

00:10:51.740 --> 00:10:54.340
with the phrases that they used
over and over again.

00:10:54.380 --> 00:10:56.860
So when they turned up the BS
in a conference call,

00:10:56.860 --> 00:10:58.700
she tuned out and tried to play bingo.

00:10:59.340 --> 00:11:01.100
B: Think outside the box.

00:11:01.140 --> 00:11:04.500
I: change agent. N: paradigm shift, right?

00:11:06.220 --> 00:11:09.100
So these are things for us to think about.

00:11:09.140 --> 00:11:14.180
That if we're using BS, a lot of it,
in our business speak,

00:11:14.220 --> 00:11:18.620
it may be like Shakespeare said,
"sound and fury, signifying nothing."

00:11:20.100 --> 00:11:24.140
Which brings me to my final takeaway
of how to take BS out of business speak.

00:11:24.180 --> 00:11:26.740
Let's stick with Shakespeare.

00:11:26.740 --> 00:11:29.820
Shakespeare has given us
some great BS speak

00:11:29.820 --> 00:11:34.660
and stuff that allows itself to exist
outside of business speak

00:11:34.660 --> 00:11:36.300
in the English language.

00:11:36.340 --> 00:11:37.620
"Wild goose chase."

00:11:37.620 --> 00:11:40.260
Think about this.
Kind of a weird reference.

00:11:40.300 --> 00:11:41.740
That's from Shakespeare.

00:11:41.740 --> 00:11:45.780
"Break the ice," "in a pickle" --
all from Shakespeare.

00:11:45.780 --> 00:11:49.260
The good news is when we hear
references like this from Shakespeare,

00:11:49.300 --> 00:11:50.820
a lot of us in the world

00:11:50.820 --> 00:11:54.220
at least have a little bit
of an experience with Shakespeare.

00:11:54.260 --> 00:11:56.940
And we have context clues,
we can figure it out.

00:11:56.940 --> 00:11:59.500
But I want you to think
of other cultural touchpoints

00:11:59.540 --> 00:12:01.340
that aren't as common as Shakespeare.

00:12:01.380 --> 00:12:05.220
I'm going to put up some phrases
that are commonly used in America,

00:12:05.260 --> 00:12:08.940
and if you know its common origin,
just yell it out.

00:12:08.940 --> 00:12:10.380
If you're wrong, it's OK.

00:12:10.420 --> 00:12:12.020
We'll edit it out of the video.

00:12:12.060 --> 00:12:14.460
(Laughter)

00:12:14.500 --> 00:12:19.060
"Swing for the fences,"
"out of left field," "rain check,"

00:12:19.100 --> 00:12:22.060
“off base,” “pinch hitter,”

00:12:22.100 --> 00:12:23.860
“right off the bat,” anybody know?

00:12:23.860 --> 00:12:25.140
(Audience) Baseball!

00:12:25.180 --> 00:12:26.940
Baseball! There you go.

00:12:27.940 --> 00:12:31.380
I'm going to put up
another list of phrases.

00:12:31.420 --> 00:12:33.820
Again, you tell me
what the common origin is.

00:12:34.500 --> 00:12:36.500
"Blind leading the blind,"

00:12:36.540 --> 00:12:38.300
"by the skin of your teeth,"

00:12:38.340 --> 00:12:39.940
"fly in the ointment,"

00:12:39.940 --> 00:12:41.460
"writing is on the wall."

00:12:43.100 --> 00:12:44.340
(Audience) The Bible.

00:12:44.380 --> 00:12:45.660
The Bible.

00:12:45.660 --> 00:12:47.140
I suppose you have a Bible.

00:12:47.180 --> 00:12:51.140
You're reading scripture between speakers,
like good Christians. OK.

00:12:51.180 --> 00:12:52.420
Yeah, you're right.

00:12:52.460 --> 00:12:55.460
"No rest for the wicked"
also comes from the Bible.

00:12:55.500 --> 00:12:57.220
Here's my point.

00:12:57.260 --> 00:13:00.860
Think of the global economy now.

00:13:00.860 --> 00:13:03.860
And the majority of people on this Earth

00:13:03.860 --> 00:13:06.740
don’t have daily experience
with American baseball,

00:13:06.740 --> 00:13:08.260
and they don't read the Bible.

00:13:10.020 --> 00:13:12.020
But you're sitting in a business meeting,

00:13:12.060 --> 00:13:14.660
saying, "We have no time,
no rest for the wicked."

00:13:15.380 --> 00:13:16.780
"The writing is on the wall."

00:13:18.100 --> 00:13:19.980
Do they know what you're talking about?

00:13:21.820 --> 00:13:24.820
Clap your hands if you've ever heard
the American phrase

00:13:24.820 --> 00:13:26.700
"drinking the Kool-Aid."

00:13:26.700 --> 00:13:28.900
(Applause)

00:13:28.900 --> 00:13:32.380
Great. Anybody who clapped their hands,
what does it mean?

00:13:34.660 --> 00:13:36.420
(Laughter)

00:13:37.860 --> 00:13:39.460
It's a --

00:13:39.500 --> 00:13:40.780
What's that?

00:13:40.780 --> 00:13:42.740
(Unclear voice from the audience)

00:13:44.540 --> 00:13:46.940
We'll get there. We'll get there.

00:13:46.940 --> 00:13:48.220
(Laughter)

00:13:48.260 --> 00:13:50.540
You've read my script.
You know where I'm going.

00:13:51.500 --> 00:13:55.500
What does "drinking the Kool-Aid" --
What does that mean?

00:13:55.540 --> 00:13:57.420
(Audience) Succumbing to peer pressure.

00:13:57.460 --> 00:13:59.900
Succumbing to peer pressure.
It's kind of in there.

00:13:59.900 --> 00:14:01.980
It's accepting something without question.

00:14:02.020 --> 00:14:05.100
Just sort of like I did with you guys,
saying, "Stomp your feet."

00:14:05.100 --> 00:14:07.940
You're just like, "Sure, whatever," right?

00:14:07.940 --> 00:14:09.860
Yes. And to the balcony's response,

00:14:09.860 --> 00:14:12.300
here's the origin story
of "drinking the Kool-Aid."

00:14:12.340 --> 00:14:14.020
And the reason why I bring this up

00:14:14.060 --> 00:14:17.340
is because it's important to know
the origin story of some of our BS.

00:14:17.380 --> 00:14:19.860
Jim Jones is a cult leader. He's adorable.

00:14:19.860 --> 00:14:23.060
1978. He has a great idea.

00:14:23.100 --> 00:14:27.260
All of his hundreds of followers,
he's going to give them a delicious drink.

00:14:27.300 --> 00:14:29.260
That's wonderful of Jim Jones to do.

00:14:29.300 --> 00:14:31.500
He flavors it with something
called Flavor Aid,

00:14:31.540 --> 00:14:32.980
which is popular at the time.

00:14:33.020 --> 00:14:34.460
It's later confused in history

00:14:34.500 --> 00:14:37.540
with the more popular flavored drink
in America called Kool-Aid.

00:14:38.340 --> 00:14:41.740
But Jim made a bad mistake
with that Kool-Aid, man.

00:14:41.740 --> 00:14:43.540
He'd laced it with cyanide.

00:14:44.620 --> 00:14:47.660
Hundreds of followers
drink the cyanide and die.

00:14:48.260 --> 00:14:52.140
1978. That's the origin story
of "drinking the Kool-Aid."

00:14:52.900 --> 00:14:55.940
The reason I bring that up
is because a European audience,

00:14:55.940 --> 00:14:57.500
you're like, "What is Kool-Aid?"

00:14:58.860 --> 00:15:01.220
Also, if you're born beyond 1978,

00:15:01.260 --> 00:15:03.340
you may not even know what this is.

00:15:03.380 --> 00:15:05.460
Who is this guy?
What are you talking about?

00:15:05.500 --> 00:15:07.780
Mass suicide? A cult with Kool-Aid?

00:15:07.780 --> 00:15:09.380
"Drinking the Kool-Aid."

00:15:09.420 --> 00:15:12.180
And there are other BS phrases
that fall in this category.

00:15:12.220 --> 00:15:14.380
We've lost the origin story,
it gets muddied.

00:15:14.420 --> 00:15:16.620
Think of TED Talk. The internet.

00:15:16.620 --> 00:15:20.340
If you type “who founded TED?”
or “where did TED come from?,”

00:15:20.380 --> 00:15:22.500
it's a person on the internet.

00:15:23.700 --> 00:15:25.900
It's Technology, Entertainment, Design.

00:15:25.900 --> 00:15:28.420
That's what TED Talks stands for.

00:15:28.460 --> 00:15:30.900
Think of the BS phrase "piggyback."

00:15:30.900 --> 00:15:34.180
You ever seen two pigs
with one pig on its back?

00:15:34.220 --> 00:15:35.420
(Laughter)

00:15:35.460 --> 00:15:37.540
I haven't either. Here's why.

00:15:37.580 --> 00:15:41.020
Because the origin story of this phrase
is actually "pick back"

00:15:41.060 --> 00:15:42.900
or "pick pack."

00:15:42.900 --> 00:15:46.260
But it was so commonly
misstated as "piggyback"

00:15:46.300 --> 00:15:51.580
that that mispronunciation was adopted,
and now it means “piggyback.”

00:15:52.420 --> 00:15:55.100
And pigs are like,
"What the hell are you talking about?"

00:15:55.140 --> 00:15:56.140
(Laughter)

00:15:56.180 --> 00:15:57.820
And there are lots more like this.

00:15:57.820 --> 00:16:01.180
Because we're up against lunch,
I won't bore you with an origin story.

00:16:01.220 --> 00:16:02.780
It's a true one, it's horrible,

00:16:02.780 --> 00:16:06.180
about the BS phrase "blowing smoke
up your ass," for example.

00:16:06.220 --> 00:16:07.460
But I won't go there.

00:16:07.500 --> 00:16:09.900
(Laughter)

00:16:09.900 --> 00:16:12.780
Here's the takeaway out of all of this,

00:16:12.780 --> 00:16:16.420
is that BS terms
can get lost in translation.

00:16:16.460 --> 00:16:19.500
And I want you to think
of some of your own cultural BS

00:16:19.540 --> 00:16:21.380
that we use around the world.

00:16:21.420 --> 00:16:25.540
For example, in America,
if a company is without money,

00:16:25.580 --> 00:16:27.060
you might say it's broke.

00:16:27.100 --> 00:16:30.300
In Italy, you would say
it's "in the green."

00:16:30.340 --> 00:16:33.460
In Spain, you would say
it's "without white," right?

00:16:33.500 --> 00:16:36.060
In America, if you're doing
a very easy task,

00:16:36.100 --> 00:16:38.020
you might say it was "a piece of cake."

00:16:38.060 --> 00:16:41.380
In New Zealand, you'd say
"Bob's your uncle," right?

00:16:42.420 --> 00:16:45.740
If you have a very hard task
that will never happen,

00:16:45.740 --> 00:16:47.420
in America you would probably say,

00:16:47.460 --> 00:16:49.820
"Oh, it'll happen when pigs fly."

00:16:49.820 --> 00:16:51.980
In the Netherlands,
a Dutch worker would say,

00:16:52.020 --> 00:16:54.180
"When the calves dance on the ice."

00:16:54.220 --> 00:16:55.220
(Laughter)

00:16:55.260 --> 00:16:57.140
And in Russia they would say,

00:16:57.180 --> 00:16:59.540
"When a crayfish whistles
from the mountaintop."

00:17:00.340 --> 00:17:02.260
It means the same thing.

00:17:02.300 --> 00:17:05.740
In Japan, if you are doing a work task
and you need help,

00:17:05.740 --> 00:17:07.180
you're desperate for anybody.

00:17:07.220 --> 00:17:09.460
You may say, "I'll adopt cat paws."

00:17:11.260 --> 00:17:16.060
In America, if you are given a task
that is one day over a long-term project

00:17:16.100 --> 00:17:17.900
and man, you're just getting started,

00:17:17.900 --> 00:17:20.140
you may say that
it’s “a drop in the bucket.”

00:17:20.180 --> 00:17:22.700
In China, they have
the same idea communicated

00:17:22.700 --> 00:17:26.820
through the phrase of
"nine cows and only one cow thread."

00:17:29.060 --> 00:17:32.940
So those are examples
of cultural differences with BS.

00:17:32.940 --> 00:17:34.940
And I'd be remiss to leave you for lunch

00:17:34.940 --> 00:17:39.900
without calling out
some of the regional fondness for food

00:17:39.900 --> 00:17:42.180
in your BS, in the German language.

00:17:42.820 --> 00:17:45.820
Think of yourself, when you're
in a business meeting in Germany

00:17:45.820 --> 00:17:47.940
or in Austria, with the German language,

00:17:47.940 --> 00:17:51.900
if you want to add your two cents
in America, you'd say "two cents."

00:17:51.900 --> 00:17:54.100
Here you say, "I want to add my mustard."

00:17:54.140 --> 00:17:55.380
(Laughter)

00:17:55.420 --> 00:17:58.300
OK? And once you get that mustard,

00:17:58.340 --> 00:18:02.460
you want to be “clear as dumpling broth”
about what you’re trying to say, right?

00:18:02.500 --> 00:18:04.420
You certainly don't want to come across

00:18:04.460 --> 00:18:07.420
as somebody who has “tomatoes
on their eyes,” you know?

00:18:07.460 --> 00:18:09.820
And the last thing
you want to do in that scenario

00:18:09.820 --> 00:18:12.860
is you don’t want to “talk around
the hot porridge.”

00:18:12.860 --> 00:18:15.500
OK? Which is the equivalent
of “beating around the bush”

00:18:15.500 --> 00:18:16.660
with American BS.

00:18:17.380 --> 00:18:20.740
I've given you a lot of bullshit
over a short amount of time,

00:18:20.740 --> 00:18:23.220
so I'm going to wrap it up
with a little BS language

00:18:23.260 --> 00:18:25.060
and we'll recap here.

00:18:25.100 --> 00:18:28.140
In BS, what I am trying to say

00:18:28.180 --> 00:18:32.260
is disambiguate the net net
of this dog and pony show

00:18:32.300 --> 00:18:33.540
for you thought leaders.

00:18:33.580 --> 00:18:34.780
(Laughter)

00:18:34.780 --> 00:18:37.900
And I'm going to call out
the elephant in the room

00:18:37.900 --> 00:18:39.860
with the three takeaways here.

00:18:39.860 --> 00:18:44.900
First takeaway: use BS
to be inclusive, not exclusive.

00:18:44.900 --> 00:18:48.940
Second takeaway is to make sure
that you express fresh ideas

00:18:48.940 --> 00:18:50.620
without stale business cliches

00:18:50.620 --> 00:18:53.700
or big words that maybe your audience
doesn't really understand.

00:18:53.700 --> 00:18:55.340
And then finally appreciate

00:18:55.380 --> 00:18:58.220
that a lot of our BS
is lost in translation

00:18:58.260 --> 00:19:00.020
depending on the audience.

00:19:00.060 --> 00:19:03.420
If we can all do this,
starting at lunchtime,

00:19:05.060 --> 00:19:08.500
think of how little less bullshit
there would be in the world.

00:19:09.500 --> 00:19:11.460
And what a wonderful thing that would be.

00:19:11.500 --> 00:19:12.780
Thank you very much.

00:19:12.780 --> 00:19:16.820
(Applause)

